  "description": "Four novellas of the secret war in Republican-era China by Bi Yu. The title story follows Lin Nansheng, a Nationalist intelligence agent whose loyalties are remade across the war against Japan and the civil war that follows; the companion stories -- The Postman, Potassium Cyanide, and Rouge -- move through the same world of Shanghai spycraft, occupation, and divided allegiance. Annotated English translation with footnotes and a glossary for readers with no background in modern Chinese history.",
  "_source_note": "Source: digital EPUB of 叛逆者 (Bi Yu), People's Literature Publishing House, first published June 2020. A collection of four novellas: 叛逆者 (The Rebel, installments 1-14), 邮差 (The Postman, installments 1-15 plus a 后记/afterword coda), 氰化钾 (Potassium Cyanide, 1-10), and 胭脂 (Rouge, 1-11). The source numbers these as 51 continuous chapters (第1章..第51章); modeled here as 51 translation units grouped into four parts. Three spine documents are NOT chapters and are excluded: Text/cover_page.xhtml (cover image, reused as the EPUB cover), Text/part0000.xhtml (版权信息 copyright/imprint page), and Text/part0001.xhtml (目录 source table of contents, regenerated by the build). No source has its own footnotes/endnotes (grepped: none present). One image in the source: the cover.",
  "translator_note": [
    "This is an English translation of <i>叛逆者</i> (<i>The Rebel</i>) by Bi Yu, a collection of four novellas of the secret intelligence war in China during the war against Japan and the civil war that followed. It was made from the digital source EPUB (People's Literature Publishing House, 2020).",
    "The stories are set in the world of Republican-era Shanghai spycraft: the Nationalist secret services, the Wang Jingwei collaborationist regime and its intelligence apparatus, the foreign concessions, and the occupation. Footnotes supply the historical people, places, institutions, and customs a reader with no background in the period would otherwise miss; the glossary collects recurring terms and names. Where the source states something a historian would dispute, the translation renders it faithfully and the note records the discrepancy.",
    "Register aims at natural, literary contemporary English while preserving the original's restraint and its shifts between narration and terse dialogue. Ambiguities and any digitization glitches in the source are handled visibly rather than smoothed over."
  ],
